from curriculum_library import dossier

dossier(31,'工作方式','A hybrid-work experiment',
'The question being tested~An accountancy firm allowed staff to work remotely on two days each week. The trial was intended to test whether focused work could improve without reducing access to colleagues. It was not a promise to close the office.||Choosing comparable measures~Managers compared turnaround times for similar cases, alongside client complaints and staff wellbeing. Counting messages sent would have rewarded visible activity rather than useful output. Some valuable tasks required long periods with few messages.||Unequal circumstances~Employees with quiet rooms often found remote work easier than those sharing crowded homes. The firm kept desks available every day. Flexibility meant offering workable choices, rather than requiring everyone to stay at home equally often.||What the results justified~Routine work became faster, but new recruits reported difficulty asking informal questions. The firm retained remote days while adding scheduled support for beginners. The evidence supported revising the arrangement, not claiming that one location was best for every task.',
'A new recruit reflects~During my first week at home, I spent an hour solving something a colleague could have explained in minutes. I worried that sending a message would interrupt her. The office had made such questions feel more natural.||A clearer agreement~Our team now has a daily period when a senior colleague is available for short calls. Questions outside that period are still welcome, but the arrangement removes uncertainty about whether help is accessible.||Protecting concentration~We also mark periods of focused work on the shared calendar. An urgent client issue can interrupt them; routine updates usually wait. A calendar label does not excuse ignoring a genuine emergency.||Separating work and rest~I finish by writing tomorrow\'s first task and closing the work laptop. Without a journey home, I need another signal that the working day has ended. Flexibility should not silently become permanent availability.',
'A proposed change~Manager: Next week we need everyone in on Wednesday for equipment testing. Your usual remote day can move to Thursday if your client appointments allow it.||A conflicting commitment~Employee: I have a video meeting on Thursday that requires the secure office connection. Could I work remotely on Friday instead? Manager: Yes, but update the team calendar.||Clarifying expectations~Employee: Do we need to remain online after normal hours? Manager: No. The testing team will handle evening checks. Your responsibility is to report problems before you leave on Wednesday.||Confirming the exception~Manager: This change applies only next week. We will review the regular schedule at the end of the trial, using the same measures as before.',
'A compressed-week pilot~A publishing team will work its existing weekly hours over four days for a two-month trial. It is not a reduction in total working time or an automatic pay increase.||Service coverage~Staff must choose different non-working days so customers can still reach someone throughout the week. Friday cannot become a universal day off.||An opt-out~Employees may retain their current pattern if longer days conflict with caring responsibilities. Choosing not to join will not affect eligibility for training.||A review measure~The team will compare corrections, missed deadlines and employee feedback. Finishing more pages will not count as improvement if errors increase substantially.')

dossier(32,'住房与能源','Insulation before equipment',
'Diagnosing the problem~A housing cooperative received high winter energy bills despite recently replacing its boiler. An inspection found gaps around doors and poorly insulated roof spaces. Efficient heating equipment could not prevent heat escaping through the building.||Prioritising investment~The cooperative compared several improvements by cost, expected savings and disruption. Sealing gaps was inexpensive, but roof work required contractors and temporary access restrictions. The cheapest action was useful without being sufficient on its own.||Avoiding a false comparison~Bills after the work were lower, but that winter was also milder. The cooperative compared energy use with outside temperatures before estimating savings. A lower bill alone could reflect weather or prices rather than better insulation.||Including residents~Residents received guidance about ventilation because reducing uncontrolled draughts does not remove the need for fresh air. The project combined building changes with clear advice rather than assuming technology would solve every comfort problem.',
'A tenant\'s letter~I am writing about persistent condensation in the bedroom. I first reported it on 4 November and attached photographs. The patches have returned despite following the ventilation advice supplied by your office.||Relevant observations~The problem is greatest on the external wall behind the wardrobe. I have moved the furniture away from the wall and kept the vents open. I cannot determine whether the cause is insulation, ventilation or another defect.||A specific request~Please arrange an inspection rather than sending the same general leaflet again. I can provide access on Tuesday morning or Thursday afternoon. If neither is possible, please suggest another appointment in writing.||A reasonable boundary~I am not asking you to confirm a diagnosis before inspecting the room. I would, however, appreciate a timetable for investigation and a contact person who can update me if the appointment changes.',
'A survey appointment~Surveyor: We need access to the loft and two external walls. The visit should take forty minutes. Tenant: Will I need to move the large wardrobe?||Preparation~Surveyor: Move small objects from the area, but do not move heavy furniture alone. We will discuss safe access when we arrive.||Scope of the visit~Tenant: Will you repair the insulation immediately? Surveyor: This is an inspection. We will send recommendations and a quotation after reviewing the measurements.||Reporting results~Surveyor: Expect the written report within five working days. If we find an urgent safety issue, we will contact you sooner rather than wait for the report.',
'A solar-panel offer~A community scheme invites households to request a roof assessment. Registration is free and does not commit anyone to purchasing equipment. Installation prices depend on the final survey.||Suitability~Shade, roof condition and orientation affect output. A neighbor\'s successful installation does not establish that another roof will perform equally well.||Understanding savings~Published examples assume particular electricity use and prices. Households should compare those assumptions with their own circumstances before treating the estimate as a forecast.||After installation~The guarantee covers specified equipment faults, not every possible reduction in output. Customers receive separate instructions for maintenance and reporting problems.')

dossier(33,'数字服务','When services move online',
'A convenient change~A council introduced online applications for parking permits. Many residents appreciated being able to apply outside office hours. However, fewer visits to the counter did not prove that every resident found the new process easier.||People missing from the data~Some applicants lacked internet access, while others struggled to upload documents from a phone. Abandoned applications were initially counted only as unfinished sessions, making the scale of difficulty hard to identify.||Adding supported access~The council trained library staff to help residents use public computers. Staff could explain the process but would not choose answers or retain copies of personal documents. Assistance needed clear boundaries as well as availability.||Measuring inclusion~The review compared completion rates, waiting times and requests for help. A telephone option remained for exceptional cases. Digital convenience and an alternative route could coexist rather than being treated as mutually exclusive goals.',
'An application guide~Before starting, check that you have proof of address and the vehicle details. A document must show your current address. A recent document with an old address may still be unsuitable.||Saving progress~You can save an incomplete application and return within fourteen days. Saving is not submission. The confirmation screen appears only after you review the information and select the final submit button.||Correcting mistakes~If you notice an error after submission, contact the permit team with your reference number. Do not send a second application unless instructed, because duplicate requests may delay processing.||Protecting documents~When using a public computer, sign out and remove downloaded copies of your documents. Closing a browser tab may not delete files stored on the device. Ask staff if you need help locating them.',
'A support call~Resident: I uploaded my bill, but the application still says incomplete. Adviser: Did you select submit after uploading? Resident: I saved it because I thought that finished the process.||Next steps~Adviser: Open the saved application and review the final page. It should show a declaration and a submit button. You will then receive a reference number.||A missing message~Resident: What if no email arrives? Adviser: Check the confirmation screen and note the number. You can use it when contacting us, even if the email is delayed.||A security reminder~Adviser: Do not read your password to me. I can explain the steps without knowing your login details.',
'A school payment portal~Parents can now pay for trips through the school portal. Families needing another payment method should contact the office privately; children will not be excluded for lacking online access.||Permission remains separate~Payment does not replace parental permission. Both the consent form and payment must be received before a place is confirmed.||Refund arrangements~If the school cancels a trip, payments are refunded to the original payment method. A family\'s withdrawal follows the conditions stated on that trip\'s information sheet.||Shared devices~Parents using a shared computer should sign out after payment. The school will never ask them to send card details in an ordinary email.')

dossier(34,'城市绿地','The value of a small park',
'Competing proposals~An unused council plot could become either parking spaces or a small park. Local shops supported easier vehicle access, while residents requested somewhere to sit outside. Both groups described genuine needs rather than simply opposing improvement.||Looking at actual use~A survey counted nearby parking occupancy at different times. Several spaces were available during weekdays but scarce on Saturdays. A single morning count would have missed the busiest period.||Designing for more users~The park proposal included shade, level paths and benches with backs. These features mattered to older visitors and people with limited mobility. An attractive drawing without accessible routes would not provide equivalent benefits.||Acknowledging tradeoffs~The final design kept a small loading area while reducing the number of proposed parking spaces. It did not satisfy every preference fully. The council explained what evidence had shaped the compromise and scheduled a review after opening.',
'A resident\'s observation~I began using the new park during lunch breaks. It was quieter than the main street, although delivery vehicles still used the nearby loading area. The park did not remove all traffic noise.||An overlooked detail~The first benches faced direct afternoon sun. Several visitors stood under a tree instead. We suggested moving one bench rather than planting a tree that would take years to provide shade.||Maintenance matters~After heavy rain, water collected near the gate. The council cleared a blocked drain and asked users to report recurring flooding. Good initial design still required regular maintenance.||Beyond opening day~The launch attracted a large crowd, but daily use was a more useful measure of lasting value. We asked for counts across different seasons before drawing conclusions about the park\'s success.',
'A consultation discussion~Planner: The proposed path crosses the lawn diagonally. Resident: People already take that route to the bus stop. Will the surface be suitable for wheelchairs?||A design response~Planner: We propose a firm, level surface with shallow drainage channels beside it. The detail still needs an access review before approval.||Lighting concerns~Resident: Could bright lights disturb nearby bedrooms? Planner: The current design directs light downwards and switches to a lower level late at night.||Recording feedback~Planner: Please mark specific problem locations on the plan. General support is welcome, but precise comments help us change the design where necessary.',
'A tree-planting scheme~Residents may request a free street tree, but the council first checks underground services and pavement width. A request does not guarantee a tree outside the applicant\'s property.||Species selection~The council chooses species suited to local conditions. Residents can express preferences, but cannot demand a species whose roots or mature size would create problems.||Initial care~Volunteer watering is encouraged during dry weather. Pruning remains the responsibility of trained staff. Residents should report damaged branches rather than cut them themselves.||Long-term evaluation~The scheme records survival after three years, not only the number planted. A large planting total would be misleading if many trees died soon afterwards.')

dossier(35,'媒体与证据','Reading beyond a headline',
'A striking claim~A headline announced that a new study method doubled examination performance. The article later explained that the comparison involved a small voluntary group. The headline omitted information essential to judging how widely the result might apply.||The comparison group~Students who joined the trial were already attending optional revision sessions. Their motivation may have differed from that of non-participants. Without considering this difference, the improvement could too easily be attributed entirely to the method.||What was measured~The study measured scores on a short test immediately after practice. It did not measure recall months later or performance in a different subject. Evidence for one outcome should not silently become a claim about all learning.||A proportionate conclusion~The method might still be useful. A cautious reader can recognize a promising result while asking for larger studies and clearer comparisons. Questioning the strength of evidence is different from dismissing every new idea.',
'A shared article~A friend sent me a report about a city reducing traffic. Before forwarding it, I checked whether the article described a permanent policy or a short trial. The distinction affected how confidently I could describe the outcome.||Following the source~The report linked to council figures. Those figures covered the city center, whereas the headline referred to the entire city. Traffic may have moved to other streets outside the measured area.||Checking the dates~The comparison used a holiday week and an ordinary working week. Travel patterns could differ even without a policy change. This did not prove the policy failed, but it limited the comparison.||Writing a fair summary~I told my friend that central traffic had fallen during the trial, while wider effects remained uncertain. A useful summary preserved the result and its limitations rather than choosing only the most dramatic interpretation.',
'A newsroom conversation~Editor: Your draft says complaints fell by half. How many complaints were there originally? Reporter: Six, compared with three this month.||Adding context~Editor: Include the actual numbers. A percentage alone makes a small change sound more precise than it is. Were opening hours the same? Reporter: No, the office closed for a week.||Revising the claim~Editor: Then mention that too. We cannot assume better service caused the entire reduction. Ask whether complaints arrived through another channel.||The deadline~Reporter: Shall I delay publication? Editor: Publish the verified facts today, but leave out the unsupported conclusion until we have more evidence.',
'A product survey~A company reports that ninety percent of respondents liked its new app. The survey was offered only to customers who had used the app for at least a month.||Missing users~People who stopped using it earlier were not invited. Their absence may affect the result, especially if they left because they found the app difficult.||Question wording~The survey asked whether users liked any feature, rather than whether they preferred the app overall. These questions do not measure the same judgement.||A reasonable inference~The result shows that many continuing users found something useful. It does not establish satisfaction among all people who originally downloaded the app.')

dossier(36,'教育与公平','Who can attend evening classes?',
'A participation gap~A college offered inexpensive evening courses, yet attendance from nearby parents was low. Staff initially assumed that these residents lacked interest. Interviews revealed that childcare and transport were more important barriers than the course fee.||Testing support~The college tested an earlier start time and partnered with a childcare provider. Places remained limited, so support was offered through a transparent application process. Lowering fees alone would not have addressed the same problem.||A scheduling tradeoff~An earlier start helped some parents but conflicted with the end of other learners\' working days. The college added one later group rather than assuming that a single timetable could serve everybody.||Evaluating access~Staff monitored applications, attendance and completion separately. More applications indicated interest, but regular attendance showed whether practical barriers had actually been reduced. Completion still depended on teaching and support during the course.',
'A learner\'s account~I wanted to improve my English for work, but my shift ended after the nearest class began. Online lessons seemed convenient until I discovered that live sessions also followed a fixed timetable.||Comparing flexibility~I chose a course combining recorded explanations with one weekly discussion. The recordings let me choose when to study; the discussion provided practice I could not obtain simply by watching videos.||An unexpected challenge~Without a daily classroom, it was easy to postpone work. I arranged a regular study period and asked a classmate to discuss one task each weekend. Flexibility required planning rather than removing the need for it.||Defining progress~After two months I could explain routine work problems more clearly. I still struggled with unfamiliar accents. My improvement was specific, and did not mean I had mastered every part of the language.',
'A course enquiry~Caller: Your brochure says the course is flexible. Can I complete everything whenever I want? Adviser: The reading tasks are self-paced, but discussion sessions take place on Tuesday evenings.||A work conflict~Caller: I work every other Tuesday. Adviser: We also offer a Thursday group. You must select one group for the term rather than changing every week.||Checking support~Caller: Is feedback included? Adviser: Yes, on four written assignments. Additional individual lessons are not included in the fee.||Making the choice~Caller: Please send me the Thursday dates and cancellation terms. Adviser: I will email them before you decide whether to enroll.',
'A scholarship notice~The adult college offers a limited number of fee reductions. Applicants must describe their financial circumstances and intended course. Submitting an application does not reserve a course place.||Selection criteria~Awards consider need and eligibility, not simply the order of application. All applications received before the deadline are assessed together.||Other costs~The reduction covers tuition only. Travel, examination fees and specialist materials remain the learner\'s responsibility unless another award explicitly includes them.||Changes in circumstances~Recipients who withdraw must inform the college. Unused support may be offered to another applicant rather than transferred automatically to a different course.')

dossier(37,'健康习惯','Designing an active commute',
'A modest proposal~A workplace encouraged employees to walk part of their journey rather than setting a demanding daily exercise target. Some could leave the bus one stop early. Others already walked far enough and needed no additional challenge.||Removing practical barriers~The employer provided a dry place for shoes and coats. These small facilities mattered during wet weather. Motivational posters alone would not have solved the problem of spending a workday in damp clothes.||Avoiding a competition~Organizers avoided ranking employees by distance. Disabilities, caring responsibilities and travel routes differed. Participation was voluntary, and alternative activities were suggested without requiring employees to disclose private medical information.||Looking for sustainable change~The review asked which changes people had maintained after the campaign ended. A large total during one enthusiastic week was less informative than a smaller habit that continued. The project did not claim to replace professional health advice.',
'A personal experiment~I started walking the final ten minutes of my commute three days a week. At first I tried every day, but that proved unrealistic when I carried heavy equipment. A smaller target was easier to maintain.||Planning the route~The shortest route had a narrow pavement beside fast traffic. I chose a slightly longer path with safer crossings. The extra distance was worthwhile because I felt less rushed and more comfortable.||Using weather information~I checked the forecast and kept a light coat at work. On very wet days I used the bus for the whole journey. An occasional change did not mean abandoning the routine permanently.||Noticing limits~I felt more alert on arrival, but many factors could affect my energy. I recorded my experience as a personal observation rather than proof that every colleague would benefit in the same way.',
'A workplace discussion~Organizer: We are offering a lunchtime walk on Wednesday. It lasts twenty minutes and starts outside reception. Employee: Is the route suitable for people who walk slowly?||Adapting the activity~Organizer: Yes. We have a shorter route and will not leave anyone behind. You may also return early if you need to.||A weather question~Employee: Will it happen if it rains? Organizer: Light rain is fine, but we will cancel for severe weather. Check the notice at eleven.||Participation~Organizer: There is no attendance target. Join if it suits you; please do not pressure colleagues to explain why they cannot take part.',
'A swimming introduction~The pool offers beginner sessions for adults who are uncomfortable in deep water. Participants start in the shallow area with an instructor. The course does not require previous swimming experience.||Booking information~Places are limited to eight. Participants should book the whole four-week course, because skills are introduced progressively. Individual drop-in sessions are not available.||Equipment~Bring swimwear and a towel. Basic flotation equipment is supplied. Goggles are optional and are not included in the course fee.||Support needs~Anyone requiring additional assistance should contact the pool before booking. Staff will discuss arrangements privately rather than ask participants to describe personal needs in front of the group.')

dossier(38,'环境与资源','The reusable-cup question',
'A simple-looking choice~A cafe replaced disposable cups with a returnable-cup scheme. Customers paid a refundable deposit and could return cups at several nearby shops. The environmental value depended partly on whether cups were actually returned and reused.||Operational requirements~Returned cups needed reliable washing and transport. If too few were returned, the cafe had to keep buying replacements. Calling an object reusable did not guarantee repeated use in practice.||A pricing misunderstanding~Some customers thought the deposit was an additional permanent charge. Staff changed the signs to show the drink price separately and explain how refunds worked. Clear communication affected participation as much as the cup design.||Comparing fairly~The cafe recorded return rates and washing requirements before making broad claims about waste reduction. Different materials and local systems could produce different results. The scheme needed evaluation rather than an environmental label alone.',
'A customer perspective~I liked the idea of a returnable cup but often bought coffee while travelling away from the neighborhood. Returning to the same shop was inconvenient. The scheme became more useful when another participating shop opened near my workplace.||Remembering the return~I put the empty cup beside my bag after washing it at home. The shop still washed returned cups according to its own hygiene procedure. My cleaning did not replace that requirement.||An unclear refund~One morning a new employee offered store credit instead of returning the deposit. I showed the published policy and asked for clarification. The manager corrected the mistake and added refund training for new staff.||A practical preference~I now use the scheme when I can return the cup easily. On other journeys I bring my own cup. No single arrangement fits every trip equally well.',
'At the counter~Customer: Is the extra pound a charge for the cup? Barista: It is a deposit. We refund it when you return the cup to any participating shop.||Checking conditions~Customer: Do I need the receipt? Barista: No, the scheme\'s mark on the cup identifies it. The cup must not be cracked or missing its lid.||A different branch~Customer: Can I return it at the station? Barista: Only if that shop displays our return symbol. Not every cafe with the same brand has joined yet.||What happens next~Barista: We collect returned cups for commercial washing. Please empty remaining liquid before putting yours in the return box.',
'A container library~The market lends sturdy containers for takeaway meals. Borrowers register a contact number and return containers within seven days. Registration is free.||Late returns~A reminder is sent after seven days. A replacement charge applies only if the container remains missing after the further period stated in the agreement.||Acceptable returns~Containers may show normal signs of use. Deep cracks must be reported so damaged items can be removed from circulation rather than lent again.||Measuring success~Organizers record completed borrowing cycles, not just registrations. A large number of registered users says little about how often containers replace disposable packaging.')

dossier(39,'求职与培训','Experience that can be explained',
'An unfocused application~A job applicant listed many duties from previous roles but gave few examples of results. The employer needed evidence of handling customer problems, not merely a statement that the applicant had worked in customer service.||Selecting relevant evidence~The applicant chose an incident involving a delayed order. She described the customer\'s concern, the options she checked and the revised arrangement she confirmed. This made her contribution visible without exaggerating her authority.||Acknowledging teamwork~A colleague had arranged the replacement delivery. The applicant explained her own role in communication and follow-up rather than claiming the entire solution. Specific credit made the account more credible.||Preparing for questions~She practised explaining what she would change next time. A memorized success story was less useful than being able to discuss judgement, limits and learning when the interviewer asked an unexpected question.',
'A skills inventory~When I considered changing jobs, I first listed tasks I could demonstrate. I separated familiarity from independent competence. Having watched someone use accounting software did not mean I could manage accounts without support.||Finding a gap~Several vacancies required spreadsheet skills beyond my experience. I chose a short practical course and built a sample budget. The project gave me something concrete to discuss, although it was not professional work experience.||Checking a course claim~One provider promised employment after completion. I read the conditions and found that the promise referred to access to interviews, not a guaranteed job. The distinction mattered before paying the fee.||Presenting honestly~In applications I described the course and project accurately. I also stated which tasks I still needed help with. A clear account of current ability was more useful than an impressive but unsupported label.',
'An interview task~Interviewer: Tell us about a time you explained a complicated process. Applicant: I helped new volunteers understand our stock-recording system.||Seeking evidence~Interviewer: How did you know they understood? Applicant: I asked them to complete a practice entry and explain why they chose each field.||A difficulty~Interviewer: Did everyone succeed immediately? Applicant: No. Two volunteers confused delivery date with entry date, so I added an example showing the difference.||Reflecting~Interviewer: What would you do next time? Applicant: Test the instructions with one beginner before giving them to the whole group.',
'A work-placement offer~A museum offers a six-week placement in its visitor-services team. The placement provides supervised experience but does not guarantee permanent employment.||Eligibility~Applicants must be available for two agreed days each week. Previous museum work is not required. Experience helping the public in another setting may be relevant.||Expenses~Reasonable local travel costs are reimbursed against receipts. Accommodation and ordinary meals are not covered. Applicants should consider these costs before accepting.||Assessment~Participants receive feedback on communication and reliability. The museum does not award an accredited professional qualification at the end of the placement.')

dossier(40,'阶段检查：论证与限制','A proposal for longer opening hours',
'The request~A public study center considered extending its opening hours until ten at night. Students supported the idea, while staff raised concerns about transport home and security. The proposal therefore involved operating arrangements as well as demand.||Evidence and uncertainty~An online survey attracted many positive responses, but respondents were mostly existing daytime users. Their support did not establish how often they would attend late sessions. A four-week trial was proposed to collect actual usage data.||The trial design~Two evenings per week would have extended hours. Staff would record attendance by hour and the reasons visitors gave for attending. Comparing both busy and quiet periods would help distinguish occasional demand from sustained need.||A decision rule~The center set a minimum usage level and reviewed staffing costs before launching the trial. It would also examine access and safety reports. A popular service could still require redesign if staff could not operate it safely.',
'A balanced response~Longer opening hours could help people whose jobs prevent daytime study. However, opening every evening immediately would commit limited funds before demand was clear. A trial on selected days offers a more cautious approach.||An alternative use of funds~The same budget might purchase more daytime seating or improve internet access. These options should be compared using the needs they address, not simply the number of people who prefer them in a survey.||Why averages can mislead~An average of twenty visitors could hide a crowded first hour and an almost empty final hour. Hourly attendance would support a more precise timetable, potentially preserving useful access without paying for unused time.||A qualified recommendation~I support a trial with published evaluation criteria and a clear review date. If use remains low, the center should adjust the hours rather than treat the original proposal as a promise that cannot be revised.',
'A review meeting~Director: The late sessions attracted thirty people before eight, but only six after nine. We should consider closing at nine instead of ten.||A missing detail~Researcher: Several late users worked shifts and could not arrive earlier. Their number is small, but the access benefit is significant. We should describe that tradeoff clearly.||Cost information~Finance officer: The final hour costs more because security staff receive an evening allowance. Closing earlier would release funds for another afternoon session.||The next step~Director: Prepare both options with costs and user feedback. We will decide next month, after checking whether the shift workers have any realistic alternative.',
'A mobile-library review~A rural library service is considering moving a stop from a school gate to a village hall. The hall provides shelter and level access, but is farther from several homes.||Consultation evidence~Responses from existing users favor the school gate. The review will also seek views from residents who currently cannot use the stop, rather than treating current attendance as the full measure of need.||A trial period~For one month, the vehicle will alternate between the two locations. Notices will show exact dates. Books may be returned at either stop regardless of where they were borrowed.||A final decision~Staff will consider attendance, accessibility and travel time together. No final location has been selected, and the consultation does not cancel the existing service.')
